entry.summary:
When I was still programming at university, my “testing” looked roughly like this:
std::cout << "Value: " << value << std::endl;

along with some classic debugging: setting breakpoints, inspecting variables, stepping through the code. Looking back, this was clearly not an effective approach. I don’t even want to know how many bugs went unnoticed back then. Over several professional roles, I gradually improved my testing skills. In my first job, I initially continued exactly as I had done at university. Later, I had the chance to dive deeper into testing. At that time, my workflow looked like this: implement changes, document them, integrate, deploy, and only then start testing. This naturally led to discovering bugs late, being unable to fix them right away, and shipping bugfixes only with…
